from .L import L
LESSONS={
"e-api":L(
"Calling an LLM looks like one line of code, but in production it is a network call to a slow, sometimes overloaded, metered service that returns text a piece at a time. Application fundamentals are about treating it like any other unreliable dependency: stream results, set deadlines, retry safely, count tokens and keep the conversation state in your hands.",
["Build the request: a list of messages with roles (system, user, assistant, tool) plus settings such as model, max output tokens and response format.",
 "Send it with a deadline and stream the response (server-sent events) so users see tokens immediately.",
 "On rate limits (429) or server errors (5xx), retry with exponential backoff and jitter, up to a limit; never blindly retry side effects.",
 "Record usage from the response (input, cached and output tokens) and convert to cost.",
 "Store the conversation state yourself (or use a provider's stored-state API deliberately) and trim or summarize it to fit the context budget."],
"Exponential backoff with full jitter: base 0.5 s, cap 8 s. Attempt n waits a random time between 0 and min(8, 0.5 × 2ⁿ) seconds: up to 1 s, 2 s, 4 s, 8 s, 8 s. The worst-case total wait over 5 retries is 23 s, so a 30 s request deadline still has room for the final attempt.",
["Retrying without jitter, so thousands of clients retry at the same moment and keep the provider overloaded.","Parsing free text with regexes instead of using structured output (JSON schema) modes.","Not setting max output tokens, so a runaway answer blows the latency and cost budget."],
"A travel company's chatbot timed out during a provider incident because each request waited 60 s. Setting a 15 s deadline, streaming, and falling back to a second provider after two failed attempts kept 98% of conversations working during the next incident.",
["Treat the model API as an unreliable, metered network dependency.","Stream, set deadlines, retry with jitter.","Count tokens on every call."],
"sequenceDiagram\n  participant App\n  participant API as Model API\n  App->>API: messages + settings (deadline 15s)\n  API-->>App: 429 rate limited\n  App->>App: backoff with jitter\n  App->>API: retry\n  API-->>App: stream tokens (SSE)\n  API-->>App: usage: in / cached / out tokens\n  App->>App: log cost, update state",
"Sequence diagram: the app sends messages with a deadline, gets a rate-limit error, backs off with jitter, retries, receives streamed tokens and usage, then logs cost and updates state.",
{"e-api-client-s1":"Chat APIs take a message list; older completions APIs take one text prompt; newer responses-style APIs add built-in tools and optional server-side conversation state.",
 "e-api-client-s3":"Make writes idempotent with a request ID so a retry doesn't create a duplicate order or email.",
 "e-api-client-s4":"Set a deadline per request and cancel the stream when the user leaves, so you stop paying for tokens nobody reads.",
 "e-api-msg-s1":"The system message sets rules, user messages carry requests, assistant messages hold prior replies and tool messages return tool results.",
 "e-api-msg-s3":"Decide how many tokens each part of the prompt may use (instructions, examples, history, retrieved text) and keep room for the answer."},
code="import random, time\ndef call_with_retry(send, max_tries=5, base=0.5, cap=8):\n    for n in range(max_tries):\n        try:\n            return send(timeout=15)\n        except RateLimitOrServerError:\n            time.sleep(random.uniform(0, min(cap, base * 2 ** (n + 1))))\n    raise RuntimeError('model unavailable')", lang="python"),

"e-prompt":L(
"A prompt is a job description for a very capable new hire who knows nothing about your company and takes everything literally. Clear goals, rules, examples and the right reference material produce reliable work; vague instructions and a pile of irrelevant documents produce confident guesses. Context engineering is choosing what goes in front of the model, and in what order.",
["State the task, audience and output format explicitly, ideally with a schema.",
 "Add constraints and refusal rules (for example 'If the answer is not in the sources, say so').",
 "Add 2-5 diverse examples if format or tone matters.",
 "Assemble context in a deliberate order: stable instructions first, then retrieved evidence with IDs, then the user's request.",
 "Version the prompt and test every change against an eval set."],
"A before-and-after: 'Summarize this ticket' produced summaries of varying length that missed the customer's ask 30% of the time on a 50-ticket eval. The revised prompt specified 'Return JSON {issue, customer_ask, sentiment, next_step}; customer_ask must quote the customer if possible' with two examples. On the same 50 tickets, missing asks fell to 4% and every output parsed.",
["A 4,000-word system prompt that nobody versions or diffs.","Stuffing every retrieved chunk into the prompt, burying the relevant one.","Changing several prompt parts at once without an eval, so you can't tell what helped."],
"A bank's compliance assistant must cite policy sections. The prompt requires a citation ID for every claim and an explicit 'not found in policy' answer otherwise; an automated check rejects answers whose citations don't match retrieved chunk IDs.",
["Be explicit about task, format and refusal rules.","Order context deliberately; less but relevant beats more.","Treat prompts as versioned, tested code."],
"flowchart LR\n  SYS[Stable instructions + schema] --> CTX[Context assembly]\n  EX[Examples] --> CTX\n  RET[Retrieved evidence with IDs] --> CTX\n  MEM[Memory summary] --> CTX\n  USER[User request] --> CTX\n  CTX --> LLM[Model]\n  LLM --> VAL[Schema + citation check]",
"Diagram: stable instructions, examples, retrieved evidence, memory and the user request are assembled into context for the model, whose output is validated against schema and citations.",
{"e-prompt-tech-s2":"Asking the model to reason step by step can help on multi-step problems; reasoning models already do this internally, so extra instructions may add only cost.",
 "e-prompt-tech-s3":"Self-consistency samples several answers and takes the majority, which improves accuracy on reasoning tasks at several times the cost.",
 "e-prompt-tech-s4":"Structured output modes constrain the model to a JSON schema so the result always parses.",
 "e-prompt-ctx-s2":"Models weigh recent and early content more. Put critical instructions at the start and repeat key constraints near the end of long prompts.",
 "e-prompt-ctx-s3":"When conversations get long, summarize older turns and drop stale tool output, but keep IDs, numbers and decisions verbatim."}),

"e-tok":L(
"Tokens are the unit you pay for and the unit that fills the context window, and embeddings are how text becomes searchable numbers. You rarely build either, but your choices about chunk size, which embedding model to use and how to budget the window quietly decide your costs and how well retrieval works.",
["Measure tokens on your real data, per language and document type, with the target model's tokenizer.",
 "Pick an embedding model suited to your domain and languages; embed queries and documents with the same model (or its paired query/document versions).",
 "Choose a similarity metric (usually cosine) and dimension; check whether the model supports shortening vectors (Matryoshka).",
 "Allocate the context window by priority: output reserve, fixed instructions, then retrieval, history and memory.",
 "Track cost per task, since agents make many calls."],
"Index sizing: 2 million chunks with 1,024-dimension float32 vectors need 2,000,000 × 1,024 × 4 bytes ≈ 8.2 GB for raw vectors. Truncating to 256 dimensions (if the model supports Matryoshka embeddings) gives ≈ 2.0 GB, and int8 quantization of 256 dimensions gives ≈ 0.5 GB, each at some recall cost you should measure.",
["Embedding with one model and querying with another; the vector spaces don't match.","Using huge chunks to save embedding calls, which then dilute retrieval and fill the context.","Pricing a product from English samples when users write in other languages."],
"A global support team measured tokens per ticket by language before launch. Thai and Hindi tickets used roughly twice the tokens of English for the same content with their chosen model, so they budgeted per language and picked a model with a more efficient tokenizer for those markets.",
["Measure tokens on real data before pricing.","Same embedding model for queries and documents.","Budget the window; reserve output space."],
"flowchart LR\n  TXT[Document] --> CH[Chunker]\n  CH --> EMB[Embedding model]\n  EMB --> V[(Vectors: dim x bytes)]\n  Q[Query] --> EMB\n  V --> SIM[Cosine similarity]\n  SIM --> TOP[Top-k chunks]\n  TOP --> BUD[Context budget]",
"Diagram: a document is chunked and embedded into vectors; queries use the same embedding model; cosine similarity picks top chunks that go into the context budget.",
{"e-tok-bpe-s1":"Code, URLs, numbers and rare jargon often split into many tokens, so the same page can cost very different amounts.",
 "e-tok-bpe-s4":"When text is too long, decide what to cut: oldest turns, middle of documents, or low-ranked chunks. Never cut silently.",
 "e-emb-s1":"Bi-encoders embed query and document separately (fast, used for search); cross-encoders read them together (slower, more accurate, used for reranking).",
 "e-emb-s2":"Matryoshka representation learning trains embeddings so the first N dimensions are still useful, letting you trade size for accuracy.",
 "e-emb-s5":"Some models use different prefixes or encoders for queries and documents; using the wrong one lowers recall.",
 "e-tok-x1-s1":"Stable content first (system prompt, tools) maximizes prompt-cache hits; variable content goes last.",
 "e-tok-x1-s3":"Summarize old turns and drop stale tool outputs so the window holds what matters now."},widget="tokenizer"),

"e-tools":L(
"Tool calling lets a model ask your code to do things: look up an order, run a query, send an email. The model never runs the tool itself; it emits a structured request and your code decides whether and how to execute it. Good tools are like good APIs for a smart but literal colleague: few, clearly named, strictly typed and safe to retry.",
["Define a small set of task-oriented tools, each with a clear name, description and JSON schema for arguments.",
 "The model returns a tool call with arguments; your code validates them against the schema and the user's permissions.",
 "Execute the tool with a timeout; return a concise result or an actionable error message.",
 "Feed results back to the model, which may call more tools (possibly in parallel) or answer.",
 "Stop at a maximum number of iterations, and require human confirmation for irreversible actions."],
"Tool design: instead of exposing get_customer, get_orders, get_shipments and get_refunds (four calls and lots of IDs), expose one tool get_order_status(order_id) that returns {status, carrier, eta, refund_state}. On a 100-question eval of order queries, the agent's average tool calls fell from 3.4 to 1.1 and wrong answers from 12 to 3.",
["Exposing dozens of CRUD endpoints so the model struggles to pick the right one.","Running model-generated SQL or shell commands without a permission layer or sandbox.","Returning raw stack traces (possibly with secrets) to the model as errors."],
"A bank's assistant can look up balances and prepare transfers. The transfer tool only creates a draft that the customer approves in the app; the model can never move money directly, and all tool calls are logged with arguments.",
["Fewer, sharper tools beat a mirror of your REST API.","Validate every call server-side; the model is not an authority.","Cap iterations and gate side effects."],
"sequenceDiagram\n  participant U as User\n  participant M as Model\n  participant H as Your code\n  participant T as Tool\n  U->>M: Where is order 123?\n  M->>H: call get_order_status(order_id=123)\n  H->>H: validate schema + permissions\n  H->>T: execute (timeout 5s)\n  T-->>H: {status: shipped, eta: Fri}\n  H-->>M: tool result\n  M-->>U: Shipped, arriving Friday",
"Sequence diagram: the user asks about an order, the model emits a tool call, your code validates and executes it with a timeout, returns the result, and the model answers.",
{"e-tools-des-s1":"Design tools around user tasks ('refund_order') rather than database tables ('update_row'), so the model needs fewer, safer steps.",
 "e-tools-des-s2":"The description is the model's only documentation: say what the tool does, when to use it and what each argument means.",
 "e-tools-des-s4":"Use idempotency keys so repeating a call (after a retry or a model loop) doesn't repeat the side effect.",
 "e-tools-loop-s2":"When a call fails, return a short message that says what was wrong and how to fix it, so the model can correct the arguments.",
 "e-tools-loop-s3":"Set a hard cap on tool-call rounds so a confused agent can't loop forever."},widget="agentloop"),

"e-rag":L(
"RAG (retrieval-augmented generation) is an open-book exam for the model. Instead of hoping it memorized your company's policies, you find the relevant pages and put them in front of it, then ask it to answer only from those pages and cite them. Most enterprise AI assistants are RAG systems, and most of their failures are retrieval failures.",
["Index: split documents into chunks with metadata (source, date, permissions), embed them and also build a keyword (BM25) index.",
 "Query: optionally rewrite the question, then run hybrid search (vector + keyword) with permission filters.",
 "Rerank the top candidates with a cross-encoder and keep the best few.",
 "Generate: give the model the chunks with IDs and instruct it to answer only from them, citing IDs, or to say it doesn't know.",
 "Evaluate retrieval (recall@k) and generation (faithfulness, correctness) separately."],
"Reciprocal rank fusion of keyword and vector results with k = 60: a chunk ranked 1st by BM25 and 3rd by vectors scores 1/61 + 1/63 ≈ 0.0164 + 0.0159 = 0.0323. A chunk ranked 2nd by vectors only scores 1/62 ≈ 0.0161. The first chunk wins because both retrievers agree on it.",
["Using one chunking strategy for policies, tables and code alike.","No permission filters, so users can retrieve documents they shouldn't see.","Only measuring final answers, so you can't tell whether retrieval or generation failed."],
"An IT help desk assistant answers from 40,000 knowledge-base articles. Adding BM25 to vector search fixed failures on error codes like '0x80070005', and per-article permission filters keep HR-only articles out of general answers.",
["RAG = retrieve, then answer from evidence with citations.","Hybrid search plus reranking is the strong default.","Measure retrieval and generation separately."],
"flowchart LR\n  DOC[Documents] --> CH[Chunk + metadata + ACL]\n  CH --> VI[(Vector index)]\n  CH --> KI[(BM25 index)]\n  Q[Question] --> RW[Rewrite]\n  RW --> VI\n  RW --> KI\n  VI --> F[Fuse + rerank]\n  KI --> F\n  F --> G[Generate with citations]\n  G --> A[Answer or 'not found']",
"Diagram: documents are chunked with metadata into vector and BM25 indexes; a rewritten question searches both, results are fused and reranked, and the model answers with citations or says not found.",
{"e-rag-index-s1":"Chunk by structure where possible (headings, sections, table rows) rather than fixed character counts, and keep some overlap for context.",
 "e-rag-index-s3":"Store source, date, owner and access lists with each chunk so you can filter, cite and enforce permissions.",
 "e-rag-index-s4":"Keyword search catches exact identifiers; vector search catches paraphrases. Combining them is more robust than either.",
 "e-rag-query-s3":"A cross-encoder reranker reads the question with each candidate chunk and reorders them, usually improving the top few a lot.",
 "e-rag-query-s5":"If the evidence doesn't support an answer, the system should say so instead of guessing.",
 "e-rag-fail-s1":"A fact split across two chunks may never be retrieved together. Overlap, larger sections or parent-document retrieval help.",
 "e-rag-fail-s3":"Retrieved documents can contain instructions. Treat them as data and never let them change tool permissions.",
 "e-rag-x1-s4":"Use vector search to find starting entities, then follow graph relations to collect connected facts for multi-hop questions.",
 "e-rag-x2-s3":"A grader (often a small LLM call) labels each retrieved chunk relevant or not, so the system knows when to retry or abstain."},widget="rag"),

"e-cache":L(
"Many requests repeat: the same FAQ, the same long system prompt, the same document asked about twice. Caching avoids paying full price every time. Exact caches reuse identical requests, prompt (prefix) caching reuses the provider's work on a repeated beginning of the prompt, and semantic caches reuse answers for questions that mean the same thing.",
["Use provider prompt caching first: keep the stable prefix identical across calls.",
 "Add an exact-match cache for deterministic, repeated requests.",
 "Add a semantic cache: embed the question and reuse a stored answer above a tuned similarity threshold.",
 "Compose keys with tenant, model, prompt version and tool-policy version; set TTLs.",
 "Bypass for personalized, time-sensitive or tool-using requests, and monitor hit rate next to quality."],
"Threshold tuning: on 1,000 labeled question pairs, a similarity threshold of 0.90 gives a 35% hit rate but 6% of hits return a wrong answer; 0.95 gives 22% hits with 0.5% wrong. If a wrong answer costs far more than an API call, 0.95 is the better choice even though it saves less.",
["Caching answers that depend on live data such as stock levels.","Sharing a cache across permission tiers or tenants.","Celebrating a high hit rate without checking whether cached answers are correct."],
"An e-commerce company caches answers to shipping and returns FAQs per country and per policy version. When the returns policy changes, bumping the policy version in the key invalidates all affected entries instantly.",
["Prefix caching is the free win: order prompts stable-first.","Semantic cache thresholds need an eval.","Version and tenant belong in the key."],
"flowchart LR\n  Q[Request] --> K[Key: tenant + model + prompt v + policy v]\n  K --> EX{Exact hit?}\n  EX -->|yes| R[Return]\n  EX -->|no| SEM{Semantic hit >= threshold?}\n  SEM -->|yes| R\n  SEM -->|no| LLM[Call model with cached prefix]\n  LLM --> STORE[Store with TTL]\n  STORE --> R",
"Diagram: a request builds a key with tenant and versions, checks an exact cache, then a semantic cache, and otherwise calls the model and stores the answer with a TTL.",
{"e-cache-des-s1":"The similarity threshold trades hit rate against wrong answers. Tune it on labeled pairs of questions that should and shouldn't match.",
 "e-cache-des-s2":"Include everything that changes the right answer: tenant, user role, model, prompt version and policy version.",
 "e-cache-des-s4":"Skip the cache for requests that are personalized, depend on real-time data or trigger tools.",
 "e-cache-ops-s2":"If a wrong answer gets cached, every similar question gets it. Have a way to purge entries quickly.",
 "e-cache-ops-s3":"Put the policy or prompt version in the key so changes automatically stop serving old answers."}),

"e-eval":L(
"Product evals are the test suite for an AI feature. They answer 'is this change better or worse?' with numbers instead of opinions. A good suite mixes everyday cases, hard cases and attacks, checks both answers and process (did it call the right tool?), and runs automatically on every change, blocking merges that make things worse.",
["Write slices: capability (does it do the task), safety (does it refuse what it should), style (tone, format).",
 "Collect cases from real usage, add adversarial ones, and label expected outcomes or rubrics.",
 "Grade with code where possible, with calibrated LLM judges elsewhere, and with humans for a sample.",
 "Run in CI with baselines; fail the build on meaningful regressions in critical slices.",
 "Sample production traffic, label failures and add them to the suite."],
"Factorial testing: changing model (A→B) and prompt (v1→v2) together raised accuracy from 81% to 86%. Running all four combinations on the same 400 cases showed A+v2 = 85%, B+v1 = 80%, B+v2 = 86%. Almost all of the gain came from the prompt; model B cost twice as much for 1 extra point.",
["Only happy-path demo cases in the suite.","Changing model and prompt at once and crediting the wrong one.","Flaky evals (random seeds, live data) that teams learn to ignore."],
"A legal-tech company's contract-review assistant has 600 eval cases sliced by clause type. A new prompt improved indemnity clauses but broke termination clauses; the CI gate on per-slice accuracy blocked the merge.",
["Evals are the spec and the safety net.","Slice results; aggregates hide regressions.","Block merges on critical regressions."],
"flowchart LR\n  PR[Pull request] --> CI[CI eval run]\n  CI --> S1[Capability slice]\n  CI --> S2[Safety slice]\n  CI --> S3[Style slice]\n  S1 --> CMP{Compare to baseline}\n  S2 --> CMP\n  S3 --> CMP\n  CMP -->|ok| MERGE[Merge]\n  CMP -->|regression| BLOCK[Block]\n  PROD[Production samples] --> LABEL[Label failures] --> CI",
"Diagram: a pull request triggers CI evals across capability, safety and style slices, compared to a baseline to merge or block; labeled production failures feed back into the suite.",
{"e-eval-suite-s2":"Golden cases are representative and labeled; adversarial cases try to break the system with tricky inputs or attacks.",
 "e-eval-suite-s3":"For agents, check steps too: right tool, valid arguments, no unnecessary calls.",
 "e-eval-ops-s2":"Measure how often the LLM judge agrees with human labels and adjust the rubric until agreement is acceptable.",
 "e-eval-ops-s5":"Fix the seed, freeze test data and pin model versions so the same code gives the same score."},widget="eval"),

"e-vector":L(
"Finding the nearest vectors among millions by checking every one is slow. Vector databases use approximate nearest-neighbor (ANN) indexes that check only a smart subset, like using a map of neighborhoods instead of knocking on every door. You trade a little recall for a lot of speed, and the index's knobs let you choose where on that trade-off you sit.",
["Exact search compares the query with every vector: perfect recall, cost grows linearly with data.",
 "HNSW builds a multi-layer graph; search hops from coarse to fine layers toward nearer neighbors. M sets links per node, ef sets how many candidates to explore.",
 "IVF clusters vectors; search only scans the nprobe nearest clusters.",
 "Product quantization compresses vectors into short codes to save memory, at some accuracy cost.",
 "Measure recall@k against exact search on a sample and tune the knobs to meet your latency and recall targets."],
"HNSW memory: 10M vectors × 768 dims × 4 bytes = 30.7 GB for vectors. With M = 16, the base layer stores about 2M = 32 neighbor links per node at 4 bytes each ≈ 128 bytes per vector, ≈ 1.3 GB more. Raising efSearch from 40 to 200 might lift recall@10 from 0.92 to 0.99 while roughly tripling query latency; measure on your data.",
["Never measuring recall against exact search.","Filtering by tenant after ANN search, so few or no results survive the filter.","Re-embedding documents with a new model without rebuilding the index."],
"A marketplace with 50M listings uses IVF-PQ to fit the index in memory on a few machines, and keeps an HNSW index for the 2M most active listings. Weekly recall checks against exact search on 1,000 sample queries catch degradation after data changes.",
["ANN trades recall for speed; measure both.","Filters must be built into search, not applied afterward.","Size memory before choosing a database."],
"flowchart TB\n  Q[Query vector] --> L2[Top layer: few nodes]\n  L2 --> L1[Middle layer]\n  L1 --> L0[Base layer: all nodes]\n  L0 --> C[Candidate list size ef]\n  C --> K[Top-k results]",
"Diagram: an HNSW search starts at a sparse top layer, descends through a middle layer to the base layer containing all nodes, keeps a candidate list of size ef and returns the top-k results.",
{"e-vec-ann-s2":"Higher M improves recall but uses more memory; higher ef (search) improves recall but slows queries.",
 "e-vec-ann-s3":"IVF assigns vectors to clusters; scanning more clusters (nprobe) raises recall and latency.",
 "e-vec-ann-s4":"PQ splits each vector into sub-vectors and stores a small code for each, compressing memory by 10-50× with some loss.",
 "e-vec-ops-s1":"Run the same queries with exact search and ANN, and count how many of the true top-k ANN found.",
 "e-vec-ops-s3":"Use pre-filtering or filter-aware indexes (or separate indexes per tenant) so restrictive filters don't destroy recall."}),

"e-reasoning":L(
"Reasoning models think before they answer: they generate hidden or summarized chains of reasoning, spending extra tokens (test-time compute) to solve harder problems. That makes them much better at math, code and planning, but slower and more expensive. Meanwhile model APIs now include built-in tools, caching and batch modes that change the cost and design of agents.",
["Decide which requests need reasoning (multi-step math, code, planning) and which don't (classification, simple lookups).",
 "Set reasoning effort or a thinking budget; higher budgets help hard tasks but add latency and billed tokens.",
 "When using tools with reasoning models, pass reasoning state back as the provider requires so the model keeps its train of thought.",
 "Use platform features deliberately: prompt caching for repeated prefixes, batch APIs for offline jobs, built-in search or code tools when they fit.",
 "Evaluate cost per correct answer for each setting and route easy requests to cheaper modes."],
"Thinking budget math: a task with 1,500 input tokens and a 300-token answer. With a reasoning model at $2 per 1M input and $8 per 1M output (thinking tokens are billed as output), low effort uses 1,000 thinking tokens: cost = 1,500×2/1M + 1,300×8/1M = $0.003 + $0.0104 = $0.0134. High effort uses 8,000 thinking tokens: $0.003 + 8,300×8/1M = $0.0694, about 5× more. Use high effort only where it measurably helps.",
["Using maximum reasoning effort for every request.","Dropping the reasoning items between tool-use turns, which degrades multi-step performance.","Using realtime APIs for overnight jobs that could run at batch pricing."],
"A financial analytics team routes spreadsheet-formula questions to low reasoning effort and multi-step forecasting questions to high effort. Monthly cost fell 60% versus 'always high', with no drop on their 300-question eval.",
["Reasoning helps hard multi-step tasks; it wastes money on easy ones.","Thinking tokens are billed; budget them.","Use caching and batch features on purpose."],
"flowchart LR\n  R[Request] --> C{Hard multi-step?}\n  C -->|no| FAST[Standard model / low effort]\n  C -->|yes| THINK[Reasoning model, high effort]\n  THINK --> TOOLS[Tools with preserved reasoning]\n  FAST --> OUT[Answer]\n  TOOLS --> OUT\n  JOBS[Offline jobs] --> BATCH[Batch API]",
"Diagram: requests are routed by difficulty to a standard model or a reasoning model with high effort and tools; offline jobs go to a batch API.",
{"e-reasoning-t1-s1":"Reasoning models are typically trained with reinforcement learning on tasks with checkable answers, which rewards useful step-by-step reasoning.",
 "e-reasoning-t1-s2":"Providers expose settings such as reasoning effort (low, medium, high) or a maximum thinking-token budget.",
 "e-reasoning-t1-s5":"Send easy requests to cheap, fast settings and hard ones to reasoning modes, based on rules or a classifier.",
 "e-reasoning-t2-s1":"Responses-style APIs can store conversation state on the provider side and return an ID, reducing what you resend.",
 "e-reasoning-t2-s4":"Batch APIs accept many requests in a file and return results within hours at a discount (often 50%).",
 "e-reasoning-t3-s1":"Vendors offer supervised fine-tuning, preference tuning and reinforcement fine-tuning (RFT), where a grader scores outputs.",
 "e-reasoning-t3-s5":"Managed eval tools are convenient, but keep your own golden set so you can compare across vendors."},widget="routing"),

"e-agents":L(
"An agent is a loop: the model looks at the goal and what has happened so far, decides the next action, a tool runs it, and the result comes back. That flexibility is great when the next step truly depends on what was just learned, and wasteful when the steps are known in advance. Good engineers start with a simple workflow and add autonomy only where it pays.",
["Map the task. If the steps are fixed, build a workflow or state machine; if they depend on results, consider an agent.",
 "Start with one agent with a few good tools before adding sub-agents.",
 "Run a plan-act-observe loop with a budget: maximum steps, tokens, time and cost.",
 "Wrap brittle steps (parsing, API calls) in deterministic code, and make side effects idempotent.",
 "Escalate to a human when confidence is low or the budget runs out, and evaluate agent vs workflow on the same tasks."],
"Agent vs workflow: on 200 expense-report tasks, a fixed 3-step workflow scored 91% at $0.012 per task. A free agent scored 93% at $0.045 with 4% runaway loops. A hybrid (workflow with an agent only for the 'unusual receipt' branch, 15% of tasks) scored 93% at ≈ $0.017. The hybrid kept the quality gain at a fraction of the cost.",
["Building a multi-agent swarm for what is really a form fill.","Stopping only when 'the model says done', with no step or cost limit.","Not comparing the agent with a simpler workflow on the same eval."],
"An IT operations team uses a workflow for password resets (fixed steps) and an agent for 'my laptop is slow' tickets, where diagnosis depends on what each check finds. The agent can run read-only diagnostics freely but needs approval to change settings.",
["Workflow when steps are known; agent when they aren't.","Every loop needs a budget and a stop rule.","Measure agents against simpler baselines."],
"flowchart LR\n  G[Goal + state] --> P[Plan next step]\n  P --> A[Act: tool call]\n  A --> O[Observe result]\n  O --> D{Done or budget hit?}\n  D -->|no| P\n  D -->|done| OUT[Answer]\n  D -->|budget / low confidence| H[Escalate to human]",
"Diagram: an agent loop of plan, act and observe repeats until done, or escalates to a human when the budget runs out or confidence is low.",
{"e-agents-when-s1":"Draw the decision tree. If every branch is known, code it; use the model only for the branches that need judgment.",
 "e-agents-when-s2":"A state machine with model-powered steps is easier to test and debug than a free agent.",
 "e-agents-when-s5":"Set caps on steps, tokens, wall-clock time and dollars per task.",
 "e-agents-impl-s1":"Each turn the model plans an action, the harness executes it and appends the observation to the context.",
 "e-agents-impl-s3":"Sub-agents get a narrow task and return a compact result, keeping the main agent's context small."},widget="agentloop"),

"e-prod":L(
"A demo works once on a good day. A product works thousands of times a day, including during provider outages, traffic spikes and attacks. Production hardening adds the boring parts that keep AI features alive: timeouts, fallbacks, queues, observability with costs, security on tools and a streaming UX that feels fast.",
["Reliability: deadlines, circuit breakers, fallback models, queues with backpressure, idempotent writes, load shedding.",
 "Observability: structured traces per request (prompt version, tools, tokens, cost, latency) with PII redaction, and sampled quality scores.",
 "Security: authentication and authorization on every tool, output filtering, dependency pinning, spend caps.",
 "UX: stream tokens and tool progress, support cancel and reconnect.",
 "On-call: alert on quality and cost anomalies, not only on errors."],
"Circuit breaker: if more than 50% of calls to provider A fail within a 30-second window (minimum 20 calls), open the breaker and send traffic to provider B for 60 seconds, then let a few test requests through (half-open). During a 10-minute outage at 100 requests per second, this avoids roughly 60,000 slow failed calls to A.",
["Logging raw prompts with customer data to a shared logging system.","No kill switch when a prompt change makes an agent loop on tools.","Paging only on HTTP 5xx while answer quality silently drops."],
"A food-delivery app streams support answers token by token, shows 'checking your order…' when a tool call starts, and falls back to a smaller model when the main provider breaker opens. Traces include cost, so a spike from a looping prompt was spotted and rolled back within 15 minutes.",
["Assume every dependency fails; design the fallback.","Trace tokens and dollars, with redaction.","Stream for perceived speed; alert on quality."],
"flowchart LR\n  U[User] --> GW[API + auth]\n  GW --> Q[Queue / backpressure]\n  Q --> BR{Circuit breaker}\n  BR -->|closed| PA[Provider A]\n  BR -->|open| PB[Provider B]\n  PA --> S[Stream SSE to user]\n  PB --> S\n  GW --> TR[(Traces: tokens, $, latency, redacted)]",
"Diagram: user requests pass auth and a queue to a circuit breaker that picks provider A or B; responses stream back via SSE and every request is traced with tokens, cost and latency.",
{"e-prod-rel-s1":"A timeout stops waiting; a circuit breaker stops calling a failing dependency for a while so it can recover.",
 "e-prod-rel-s3":"Queues absorb bursts; backpressure tells callers to slow down instead of letting work pile up until memory runs out.",
 "e-prod-obs-s1":"Record a trace per request with spans for model calls and tools, including model, prompt version, tokens and latency.",
 "e-prod-sec-s5":"Hard caps on spend per user, tenant and feature stop runaway loops and abuse.",
 "e-prod-x1-s1":"SSE is a one-way HTTP stream from server to browser, ideal for tokens; WebSockets are two-way, suited to voice and collaboration.",
 "e-prod-x1-s3":"Show tool calls as soon as they start, and only parse partial JSON arguments with a tolerant parser.",
 "e-prod-x1-s6":"Wire the cancel button to the server so generation stops, and let clients resume a stream after a network drop."}),
}
